// Half a Desk by the Window | English edition | Version C | 5 choices per decision point
// Standalone entry file. Every local branch rejoins the shared story.
-> start

=== start ===
It's Thursday after school. You and Xu Ran are sitting by the classroom window with a sheet of paper, a pencil, an eraser and a display card headed 'A Glimpse of Our Class'. # speaker:narrator # line:intro_01
This little card has to go up for tomorrow's open day. I thought a short introduction would be easy, but I've spent ten minutes going over the heading twice. # speaker:friend # line:intro_02
I can tell. 'A Glimpse of Our Class' looks much tidier than my maths notes. Where's the rest? We can't just invite everyone to admire your heading. # speaker:player # line:intro_03
My first attempt was 'We are a friendly, united class'. Then I realised the class next door could use it without changing a word. # speaker:friend # line:intro_04
Xu Ran turns the paper towards you. A faint line crosses out the sentence. In the bottom corner, there's a drawing of a cat sprawled across a textbook. # speaker:narrator # line:intro_05
Nice cat. But the teacher asked for one everyday moment, not a summary of the whole class. Are we making this bigger than it needs to be? # speaker:player # line:intro_06
Maybe. Knowing strangers will read it makes me want it to sound decent. We can't open with 'At lunchtime, everyone starts looking for food'. # speaker:friend # line:intro_07
That's pretty accurate, though. When you opened those biscuits today, three people in the front row turned round together. I thought the bell had gone early. # speaker:player # line:intro_08
They were just wondering why the wrapper was so loud. Apparently, in a quiet classroom, a packet of biscuits gets more attention than putting your hand up. # speaker:friend # line:intro_09
You slide the display card between you. It doesn't ask for competition results or grades. The small print simply says: write about something that happened in this classroom. # speaker:narrator # line:intro_10
Let's start with something we remember. If it doesn't work, we can rub it out. That eraser deserves more of a workout than the heading. # speaker:player # line:intro_11
Before we start writing, what do you think? When you imagine introducing our class to someone who's never been here, what comes to mind first? # speaker:friend # line:q01 # choice_point:cp01
* [I'd like people to see what we're really like.] -> cp01_o1
* [I'd like new visitors to feel welcome here.] -> cp01_o2
* [I'd focus on the little habits that make us us.] -> cp01_o3
* [I'd rather it sounded like a chat than an introduction.] -> cp01_o4
* [I'd start with a small moment I still remember clearly.] -> cp01_o5

=== cp01_o1 ===
I'd like people to see what we're really like. # speaker:player # line:cp01_o1_selected
I see what you mean: the classroom we describe should feel familiar to us. That opening I wrote could have come from just about anywhere. # speaker:friend # line:cp01_o1_reply
-> cp01_after

=== cp01_o2 ===
I'd like new visitors to feel welcome here. # speaker:player # line:cp01_o2_selected
Someone visiting for the first time might not even know where to stand. If our introduction makes this place feel less strange, it will have helped a little. # speaker:friend # line:cp01_o2_reply
-> cp01_after

=== cp01_o3 ===
I'd focus on the little habits that make us us. # speaker:player # line:cp01_o3_selected
Those habits can be hard to notice because we do them every day. But if we sat in another class, things would probably feel quite different. # speaker:friend # line:cp01_o3_reply
-> cp01_after

=== cp01_o4 ===
I'd rather it sounded like a chat than an introduction. # speaker:player # line:cp01_o4_selected
Now you mention it, my first sentence sounds like something you have to stand up straight to read. Writing the way we actually talk would feel easier. # speaker:friend # line:cp01_o4_reply
-> cp01_after

=== cp01_o5 ===
I'd start with a small moment I still remember clearly. # speaker:player # line:cp01_o5_selected
Sometimes a day seems unremarkable, but years later you still remember what was on the desk. Starting with that kind of memory makes sense to me. # speaker:friend # line:cp01_o5_reply
-> cp01_after

=== cp01_after ===
Xu Ran lays the pencil across the paper and glances towards the window. You follow their gaze to a faint water mark on the corner of the desk. # speaker:narrator # line:shared_01_01
I just remembered that rainy day early in the term. The window wasn't properly shut, and half this row got wet. Everyone had to move with their lunchboxes. # speaker:friend # line:shared_01_02
I remember. I had curry rice in one hand and my books in the other. When I got here, I realised I needed a third hand for the chair. # speaker:player # line:shared_01_03
You asked me very seriously to look after your spoon. It sounded like you were handing over something precious. I didn't dare put it just anywhere. # speaker:friend # line:shared_01_04
Lunch could wait. Dropping the spoon would've made things complicated. I can't remember who cleared some space on this desk, though. # speaker:player # line:shared_01_05
Neither can I. Someone pushed their books aside and said we could squeeze in. Then everyone started putting their lunchboxes down. # speaker:friend # line:shared_01_06
Xu Ran gestures across half the desk. That space held two lunchboxes, a carton of milk and an exercise book nobody had time to put away. # speaker:narrator # line:shared_01_07
That was my exercise book. All afternoon, I thought it smelled of curry. The person next to me asked if I was hungry again already. # speaker:player # line:shared_01_08
You had it easy. I brought soup. As soon as I sat down, I told myself not to knock the desk. I even had to laugh carefully. # speaker:friend # line:shared_01_09
So that's why you sat so straight while we talked about the new canteen counter. I thought you weren't interested and didn't want to keep asking. # speaker:player # line:shared_01_10
I was only just getting to know everyone. Even choosing a seat took ages. If you were standing there with your lunchbox and someone called you over, how would you feel? # speaker:friend # line:q02 # choice_point:cp02
* [I'd feel a bit awkward, like I might be interrupting.] -> cp02_o1
* [I'd feel relieved that I didn't have to find a seat.] -> cp02_o2
* [I'd listen for a while before joining the conversation.] -> cp02_o3
* [I'd probably laugh at how flustered everyone else was.] -> cp02_o4
* [I'd remember hearing someone say, 'Come and sit here'.] -> cp02_o5

=== cp02_o1 ===
I'd feel a bit awkward, like I might be interrupting. # speaker:player # line:cp02_o1_selected
That's how I felt: I wanted to join in, but worried I wouldn't know what everyone was discussing. Finding a seat somehow felt harder than finding my lunch card. # speaker:friend # line:cp02_o1_reply
-> cp02_after

=== cp02_o2 ===
I'd feel relieved that I didn't have to find a seat. # speaker:player # line:cp02_o2_selected
An invitation saves you from guessing whether it's all right to sit down. I remember my shoulders relaxing a little when I finally put down my lunchbox. # speaker:friend # line:cp02_o2_reply
-> cp02_after

=== cp02_o3 ===
I'd listen for a while before joining the conversation. # speaker:player # line:cp02_o3_selected
I'd listen first too, especially if everyone was already in the middle of something. Once I knew what they were discussing, joining in wouldn't feel so abrupt. # speaker:friend # line:cp02_o3_reply
-> cp02_after

=== cp02_o4 ===
I'd probably laugh at how flustered everyone else was. # speaker:player # line:cp02_o4_selected
Nobody looked particularly composed that day; even moving a chair meant checking what you were carrying. Knowing everyone was too busy to look cool helped me relax. # speaker:friend # line:cp02_o4_reply
-> cp02_after

=== cp02_o5 ===
I'd remember hearing someone say, 'Come and sit here'. # speaker:player # line:cp02_o5_selected
It was such a short sentence that the person who said it probably forgot straight away. I remember it because I could stop pretending to look for something. # speaker:friend # line:cp02_o5_reply
-> cp02_after

=== cp02_after ===
Xu Ran turns the paper over and writes 'rain', 'lunchboxes' and 'half a desk'. The words sit far apart, as if leaving room for everything piled there that day. # speaker:narrator # line:shared_02_01
I barely remember what we talked about. Except that long debate about whether the canteen potatoes counted as a main dish or just another shape of rice. # speaker:player # line:shared_02_02
Someone said we should stop talking about food while eating. That lasted thirty seconds before someone else asked if the same counter would be open the next day. # speaker:friend # line:shared_02_03
That sounds like us. Before the bell, it's lunch. After the bell, it's homework. When we can't work out the homework, it's back to lunch. # speaker:player # line:shared_02_04
After that, I felt comfortable pulling up a chair at lunchtime. I used to wait for an invitation, but sometimes everyone was just busy opening their lunchboxes. # speaker:friend # line:shared_02_05
You look down at the words on the paper. That rainy afternoon never made it into a class photo. After lunch, the desks were quickly wiped dry. # speaker:narrator # line:shared_02_06
I didn't realise you were hesitating. I was busy protecting my curry and trying not to stain anyone's homework. I barely remember what I said. # speaker:player # line:shared_02_07
Same lunch, different memories. You remember the spoon; I remember the chair. That exercise book would probably just ask us to keep our food further away. # speaker:friend # line:shared_02_08
Then it probably wouldn't approve of our story. It would demand the title 'Please Look After Your School Supplies', with a photo of the victim. # speaker:player # line:shared_02_09
Xu Ran underlines 'half a desk', then pauses. Through the paper, you can still faintly see the crossed-out opening on the other side. # speaker:narrator # line:shared_02_10
Nothing particularly exciting happened, though. A few people just squeezed in together for lunch. How would you feel about putting such a small moment on the display card? # speaker:friend # line:q03 # choice_point:cp03
* [Ordinary is fine. That's what most of our days are.] -> cp03_o1
* [I'd still worry that other people might find it boring.] -> cp03_o2
* [I think it's worth writing about the small details.] -> cp03_o3
* [I wonder whether our classmates would remember that day.] -> cp03_o4
* [I'd like to read it later and remember that day.] -> cp03_o5

=== cp03_o1 ===
Ordinary is fine. That's what most of our days are. # speaker:player # line:cp03_o1_selected
Most of the week really is like that, without anything especially dramatic happening. If we write about those moments, at least I'll recognise our class in the story. # speaker:friend # line:cp03_o1_reply
-> cp03_after

=== cp03_o2 ===
I'd still worry that other people might find it boring. # speaker:player # line:cp03_o2_selected
I worry about that too, since the reader wasn't sitting here with us. Before showing it to anyone, I'd wonder whether they'd want to read to the end. # speaker:friend # line:cp03_o2_reply
-> cp03_after

=== cp03_o3 ===
I think it's worth writing about the small details. # speaker:player # line:cp03_o3_selected
Before we talked about it, all I remembered was the rain that day. Now I can picture the lunchboxes, the chairs and that exercise book quite clearly. # speaker:friend # line:cp03_o3_reply
-> cp03_after

=== cp03_o4 ===
I wonder whether our classmates would remember that day. # speaker:player # line:cp03_o4_selected
They might recognise it immediately, or remember a completely different lunch break instead. If someone adds a detail when they read it, I'd like to hear it. # speaker:friend # line:cp03_o4_reply
-> cp03_after

=== cp03_o5 ===
I'd like to read it later and remember that day. # speaker:player # line:cp03_o5_selected
It feels easy to remember now, but the details might get mixed up over time. These few sentences could help us remember exactly which desk it was. # speaker:friend # line:cp03_o5_reply
-> cp03_after

=== cp03_after ===
Xu Ran turns the paper back over. You reread the instructions, circle 'one everyday moment' and start turning your memories into a few sentences. # speaker:narrator # line:shared_03_01
I'll try a draft. Tell me if anything sounds awkward. We don't need names; there isn't much room, and explaining what happened should be enough. # speaker:friend # line:shared_03_02
Fine. Just don't say I charged into the classroom clutching my lunchbox. I was walking slightly faster than usual, and the spoon was perfectly safe. # speaker:player # line:shared_03_03
All right, no spoon. Here's the start: 'One rainy day, half the desks by the window got wet. At lunchtime, a few classmates squeezed their lunchboxes into the space left over.' # speaker:friend # line:shared_03_04
'Someone moved their books; someone else pulled out a chair. We guarded against the spilling of our soup and debated the canteen potatoes, without reaching any conclusion.' # speaker:friend # line:shared_03_05
'The next day was dry, but we sat in the same row again. If you're looking for somewhere to sit, you could start by asking: is this seat taken?' # speaker:friend # line:shared_03_06
You take the paper and read it through. Xu Ran waits beside you, still holding the eraser, ready to change another line. # speaker:narrator # line:shared_03_07
'Guarded against the spilling of our soup' sounds awkward. How about 'tried not to spill our soup'? The rest makes sense. I can picture those desks. # speaker:player # line:shared_03_08
Done. How about 'Half a Desk by the Window' for the title? It tells people what it's about, without bringing the cat back to rescue us. # speaker:friend # line:shared_03_09
Let's use that. The cat can stay on the draft. It's kept us company this whole time; it deserves somewhere it won't be rubbed out. # speaker:player # line:shared_03_10
That last bit, 'is this seat taken', got me thinking. Even if someone really asked, I might get nervous and blurt out 'Have you eaten?' # speaker:friend # line:shared_03_11
Asking about lunch at a morning open day would fit our story. Let's practise. You be the new student walking in, and I'll try saying hello. # speaker:player # line:shared_03_12
All right. I'm by the door, not sure whether to come in. You've noticed me. What's the first thing you'd say? # speaker:friend # line:q04 # choice_point:cp04
* [There's room here. Want to sit down and join us?] -> cp04_o1
* [Would you like to look around, or sit down for a bit?] -> cp04_o2
* [We're talking about lunch break. What do you usually do?] -> cp04_o3
* [It's normal to feel lost at first. Ask me anything.] -> cp04_o4
* [Put your bag here. No need to stand while we chat.] -> cp04_o5

=== cp04_o1 ===
There's room here. Want to sit down and join us? # speaker:player # line:cp04_o1_selected
Thanks, I'll sit here; I thought all the seats in this row were taken. What are you talking about, and what have I missed so far? # speaker:friend # line:cp04_o1_reply
-> cp04_after

=== cp04_o2 ===
Would you like to look around, or sit down for a bit? # speaker:player # line:cp04_o2_selected
I'll sit for a bit; my legs are tired from wandering along the corridor. Once I've had a rest, you can tell me about the classroom. # speaker:friend # line:cp04_o2_reply
-> cp04_after

=== cp04_o3 ===
We're talking about lunch break. What do you usually do? # speaker:player # line:cp04_o3_selected
I usually relax after eating, and sometimes chat with a few classmates too. This room seems quiet; do you all stay here during lunch break? # speaker:friend # line:cp04_o3_reply
-> cp04_after

=== cp04_o4 ===
It's normal to feel lost at first. Ask me anything. # speaker:player # line:cp04_o4_selected
Then here's an easy question: is it all right to sit in this chair? I waited by the door because I wasn't sure whether it was someone's usual seat. # speaker:friend # line:cp04_o4_reply
-> cp04_after

=== cp04_o5 ===
Put your bag here. No need to stand while we chat. # speaker:player # line:cp04_o5_selected
Thanks, I've been carrying it around, and my shoulders are starting to feel it. If I can leave it here, I can finally get my water bottle out. # speaker:friend # line:cp04_o5_reply
-> cp04_after

=== cp04_after ===
The seated rehearsal ends. Xu Ran slides the draft between your desks. You read it once more and check that the awkward sentence has been fixed. # speaker:narrator # line:shared_04_01
We probably won't need a whole rehearsal tomorrow. If someone asks something, we'll answer. Better than preparing everything in my head and only managing a nod. # speaker:friend # line:shared_04_02
If they ask what's good at the canteen, we'll have plenty to say. As long as they don't expect us to guarantee tomorrow's menu. # speaker:player # line:shared_04_03
True. The card doesn't say we're an information desk. We're just sharing one small thing about our class. I nearly turned it into another big assignment. # speaker:friend # line:shared_04_04
You hold the paper steady while Xu Ran copies the final version onto the display card. At 'half a desk', they glance at the desk in front of you, then continue. # speaker:narrator # line:shared_04_05
There's room at the bottom for both our names. Otherwise, years from now, I might remember the cat and forget who actually wrote the story. # speaker:player # line:shared_04_06
You sign first. Finally, a page with something on it. No staring at a blank document tonight, and no handing in an extra-bold heading tomorrow. # speaker:friend # line:shared_04_07
You write 'Lin Cheng' at the bottom, and Xu Ran adds their name beside yours. One is larger than the other. You leave them together on the same line. # speaker:narrator # line:shared_04_08
Let's put it up before the first lesson tomorrow. I'll bring tape. Remind me to take the card out, so I don't hand it in with my maths homework. # speaker:player # line:shared_04_09
I will. Though if our maths teacher sees it, I'm sure they'll realise we don't need an equation to find the area of this half-desk. # speaker:friend # line:shared_04_10
You tuck the card titled 'Half a Desk by the Window' into your folder. The chair beside you stays where it is. You agree to put the card up together tomorrow morning. # speaker:narrator # line:ending_last # end:common
-> END
